WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:07.990
Film Courage: Matthew, what was your first film job?
Matthew Miele, filmmaker: Film job that's a good question so I

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:16.960
jumped into it pretty boldly I guess
because I studied it at Syracuse

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.710
Screenwriting and Film production, etc
but you really learn theory, you don't

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:28.240
really learn the basics of how to do it
as far as like you know onset experience.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:32.839
So I was bold enough to just say I
wanted to write a screenplay and make it

00:00:32.839 --> 00:00:39.320
and direct it and it was kind of like I
just jumped in and I wanted to be at

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:45.440
that place without kind of working as a
PA at first or working as a grip or

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.510
anything it was I came in through
actually music because music was my what

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:54.890
did they call a double major I guess and
I was composing music for commercials

00:00:54.890 --> 00:01:01.339
when I first got out of school so I had
like this weird entry and the music was

00:01:01.339 --> 00:01:07.790
what inspired me to eventually write a
script and then I made that film I

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:11.270
directed it I raised the money i
co-wrote it with a friend of mine from

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:16.490
Syracuse and we just did it and it was
this crazy ride and I'm glad we did

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:22.250
because you know it was a story that was
really close to us and we really wanted

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:26.030
to make sure we told it and the only way
to really tell a story the right way

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:31.280
when you feel near and dear to it to me
is to direct it yourself and even though

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:35.180
we had an uphill battle with the first
time director thing and cast not coming

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:39.409
on board and just people kind of like
you guys are in your mid-20s what are

00:01:39.409 --> 00:01:45.320
you doing we all felt like this was the
right way and I think the baptism by

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:51.020
fire of being unset and really learning
and you know it was my first time on a

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:58.189
film set so it was very difficult to you
know walk that tightrope of being you

00:01:58.189 --> 00:02:02.929
know immersed with the crew for the
first time as well as the talent and

00:02:02.929 --> 00:02:07.280
just trying to like
that out this was a narrative it was

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:13.220
yeah it was a thumb called everything's
Jake that start Ernie Hudson who was

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:17.900
kind enough to star in it because you
know he didn't know us from Adam and he

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:20.599
sat us down on a lunch out here I
remember we flew out because he

00:02:20.599 --> 00:02:23.480
responded the script and he's like you
guys sure you want to do this it was

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:27.560
kind of like this you know is this
possible like you have the money and

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:31.160
we're like no not yeah well we'll raise
it don't worry and you know we were just

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:35.900
kind of hustlers at the time and he
started calling friends of his like

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:41.630
Debbie Allen and Robin Givens to take
part in it and we made the movie and you

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:45.560
know we were on the streets of New York
in the dead of winter for four weeks but

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:48.680
it was about a homeless guy in New York
so the sets were built so it wasn't like

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:53.180
we had to like do anything or expense
that so we were able to do it all

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:58.190
outside and it just really worked out
and the great ending to that was that we

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:03.860
made the film no one wanted it as with a
lot of indie films I'm sure as thousands

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:07.880
of films are made and I you know only a
select hundred or what five or six

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:11.180
hundred get to the theaters every year I
think so this film was like you know

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:14.750
something we really wanted to get out
there but no one was biting eventually

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:18.920
Ernie did this radio show Whoopi
Goldberg she's and I said did you ever

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:23.299
see the film cuz she was saying oh you
know everything's Jake and she goes no

00:03:23.299 --> 00:03:26.540
you know I only watched the trailer
I said well watch the film I gave her a

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:29.630
DVD I said you know it's a really
special film over blah two weeks later

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:35.000
she brought earning on the view and then
Warner Brothers eventually took the film

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:41.329
out not in theaters but it was like this
DVD play that they did and it got out

00:03:41.329 --> 00:03:46.579
there enough and it got us to our next
film but we didn't want to give up on it

00:03:46.579 --> 00:03:49.670
it's easy to kind of like say that film
didn't work we'll shelve it but that was

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:53.359
something we wanted to like continue
with that's a great story what was the

00:03:53.359 --> 00:03:58.730
time frame between the view sort of
airing and and the finish well that's

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:04.420
the the worst part of it which was we
finished it in 99

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:09.250
and I think it didn't get a deal - like
eight years later but I didn't want to

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:15.010
do another film until like that made it
and I I didn't want to just you know say

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:19.600
okay well let's give up on it in a sense
I mean I was able to do other jobs in

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:24.190
between and it's trying to figure out
this business and to navigate it which

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:28.660
is there's no real rhyme or reason to it
it's kind of like you can come in a lot

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:32.260
of different ways and I'm sure you've
heard that from several people everyone

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:36.250
has a different experience but uh you
know and I don't think you ever really

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:40.360
arrived it's kind of you just keep
making your way and I feel like that's

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:44.680
kind of where I am right now when did
you decide on documentary filmmaking

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:49.180
your first film is a narrative mm-hmm or
did it sort of not really there wasn't a

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:54.640
decision you do an interest sparked for
something so my thinking behind it was

00:04:54.640 --> 00:05:00.130
so I did a second narrative called
eavesdrop and then that had a very hard

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:05.230
time getting out there too so I was very
discouraged because I was trying to

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:09.610
figure out well how am I gonna tell the
stories I want to tell and then you have

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:13.780
this huge mountain to climb with raising
money and the cast and you have a very

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:17.169
small amount of time to get it right
with a feature cuz it's on like a four

00:05:17.169 --> 00:05:22.479
or five week onset experience and
there's such long days and I really was

00:05:22.479 --> 00:05:26.770
discouraged with the process of
storytelling and the feature narrative

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:30.940
however from those two directing gig
because I got noticed for some writing

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:34.960
because I had written the scripts so I
came out here and I was doing some

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:39.550
writing and I got a manager and I was
working with an agency and I started

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:43.419
working with a couple of high-profile
directors on different scripts and I was

00:05:43.419 --> 00:05:47.350
helping to do a couple of things but
ultimately I got discouraged because

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:51.220
with that you sometimes they're in
turnaround a lot of things you don't get

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:55.600
credited if you work on something and
you know you're in that development

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:59.590
thing and sometimes your work doesn't
really pay off because you can be

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:03.190
working on something for so long it
never gets made so then I had this

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:07.000
frustration point where I was like I
just want to tell my stories like how

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:12.840
can I do this a lot easier in a sense
and I'm a New Yorker in my heart and I

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:17.360
as a child used to come into the city
and see the windows that

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:22.159
Goodman and a variety of different store
fronts and I remembered that as a kid

00:06:22.159 --> 00:06:25.729
and I used to look up at these windows
and say wow that's like my first

00:06:25.729 --> 00:06:29.240
approach and first encounter was
storytelling and like a three-act

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:34.250
structure you could see within just one
window and I just absorb that and then I

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:39.650
said to myself you know what I'd love to
do a film on Bergdorf's because that was

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:45.020
my first encounter with this window or
windows I approached them and they said

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:48.650
we don't allow filming and to do a whole
feature would be crazy because you'd

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:54.199
have to buy the store out and film and
it's Multi multi millions to even think

00:06:54.199 --> 00:06:57.469
about it but then I said well let me
look at your archive let me do a little

00:06:57.469 --> 00:07:01.460
more research on the place and when they
showed me their archive it was so thin

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:05.659
and I was like well you know you don't
keep a formal archive like you know I'm

00:07:05.659 --> 00:07:09.439
surprised it's not huge is a hundred
years old and they said no I said well

00:07:09.439 --> 00:07:14.719
let's do a project together let's you
know I'll be for archived with

00:07:14.719 --> 00:07:19.129
interviews and you know we'll figure it
out together and I said let's do a

00:07:19.129 --> 00:07:23.210
documentary and they said okay so for
the next two years we were rolling

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:28.430
camera and you know you can greenlight
your project like that without all the

00:07:28.430 --> 00:07:32.509
money you need you know to get it done
and also there's this protracted period

00:07:32.509 --> 00:07:37.250
where you can work on something for a
longer period of time at your own pace

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:41.599
and you don't have to have that really
concentrated thing and then at that

00:07:41.599 --> 00:07:45.710
moment and it was in like 2011 when I
started to think about that and that's

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:49.669
when Doc started to really get hot and
have this golden period which I still

00:07:49.669 --> 00:07:55.279
think we're going through and I feel
like storytelling and in a doc form is a

00:07:55.279 --> 00:08:00.319
really refreshing approach if you really
want to get your thing or film made and

00:08:00.319 --> 00:08:05.240
out there because you can literally you
know for a thousand two thousand bucks

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:09.319
have a camera start rolling and
greenlight your own project it's really

00:08:09.319 --> 00:08:13.009
about picking the right topic getting
the people in it that you want to

00:08:13.009 --> 00:08:17.000
interview or highlight and just make a
compelling story and if you stitch that

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:20.990
all together successfully you can get
something out there in a theatrical way

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:25.580
just as much as a feature narrative as
these days I mean my last four Doc's all

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:29.839
went to theaters so I feel like and they
were rated and it's like you know you

00:08:29.839 --> 00:08:32.900
feel like you're
doing it and in it but it was a

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:38.360
byproduct and a result of my frustration
with the feature side so I'm kind of

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:44.360
like in the doc world right now and I've
done about four and I'm about to do two

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:49.130
more that are already shot but I'm
entering back into the feature world now

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:53.840
because I got attached to direct this
film on Norman Rockwell to biopic on

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:57.410
that iconic artist
so I'm really excited about that so I'm

00:08:57.410 --> 00:09:01.520
kind of like entering back in but I feel
better now because I've accomplished

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:06.140
what I needed to in the doc world and I
feel like with that experience I'll

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:11.750
bring more to my directing prowess when
I get on set and do that next thing what

00:09:11.750 --> 00:09:16.070
mistakes would you say you made early on
with filmmaking whether it was with

00:09:16.070 --> 00:09:20.630
equipment you know timing whatever the
biggest mistake I ever made

00:09:20.630 --> 00:09:27.200
and it was a inadvertent one when I
first was on set as a director I didn't

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:32.000
spend time with the crew and the reason
I didn't was because I didn't want them

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:36.920
to suspect and find out that I didn't
know much and I was inexperienced and I

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:39.830
was doing this like total
fish-out-of-water like I had no clue

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:44.720
except for what I learned in school
which but in school you do little videos

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:48.890
and you're not really directing a
full-on feature so when the crew would

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:53.660
have lunch I was always trying to
separate myself because I didn't want to

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:58.250
like let on that like like Oh what have
you worked on before or you know

00:09:58.250 --> 00:10:02.120
anything question like that I had no
idea how to answer it but they saw it as

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:06.320
or at least my thinking I have no idea
never asked them but I always felt like

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:10.250
they were a little like standoffish
because they're like oh he doesn't want

00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:15.380
to eat with us or you know he wants to
spend time alone and I always felt like

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:19.070
that was it was the wrong impression I
made obviously but again it was

00:10:19.070 --> 00:10:24.620
inadvertent cuz I was trying to like
protect myself from there you know more

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:31.220
investigative questions maybe about like
if I had any background in this which I

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:35.810
didn't so I would always say and I still
today would say you know if you're on

00:10:35.810 --> 00:10:40.130
set and you're directing you should
spend time with your crew because it's a

00:10:40.130 --> 00:10:43.720
true collaboration and without all those
voices coming in and

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:46.750
helping you to immerse yourselves in the
story

00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:53.170
you can't think it's this like you know
I'm the only voice in this I'm the only

00:10:53.170 --> 00:10:57.459
storyteller it's a variety of people who
are gonna help you along the way and

00:10:57.459 --> 00:11:01.029
they all have their own voice and
creativity they're bringing to it so

00:11:01.029 --> 00:11:04.810
that's that mutual respect across the
board that I think is super important to

00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:08.680
make sure that you know I always keep in
the back of my mind and I know on my

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:12.009
next film
I'll always you know really engage more

00:11:12.009 --> 00:11:18.670
but it's so easy to get in your own head
and to kind of you know just think and

00:11:18.670 --> 00:11:24.160
brood and you know try to figure out how
you're gonna do a scene and you feel

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:27.220
like you're in it alone sometimes
because the producers are only going to

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:32.860
you when you're losing light and you're
not making your day but I feel now that

00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:41.259
I need to be a little more involved with
how the crew is feeling and you know

00:11:41.259 --> 00:11:45.579
have lunch with them and bring the
actors in on those conversations and

00:11:45.579 --> 00:11:51.160
everyone in it together rather than you
know I think a rookie director mistake

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:54.759
is to think you're in it alone and that
it's you against the world in a sense

00:11:54.759 --> 00:12:01.930
and you kind of feel like if this
doesn't go I fail which is true because

00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:06.100
it's your name on it and you're not if
you don't make your day and you go over

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:11.649
budget you could get fired or whatnot
but I do think the voices on the crew or

00:12:11.649 --> 00:12:17.589
super important to keep engaging and to
involve at an equal level as far as

00:12:17.589 --> 00:12:22.420
their creativity so that's an
interesting take on perception so at

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:25.959
what point did it click for you that
that could have been one of the issues

00:12:25.959 --> 00:12:35.110
and then you sort of I never cured it
okay I just did I was just that way on

00:12:35.110 --> 00:12:40.990
that that first set and I tried to help
it on the second set but I'm a victim of

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:47.559
my own like thoughts when I get really
intense about how I'm gonna structure a

00:12:47.559 --> 00:12:51.189
scene or I get you because I don't ever
plan much I always try to make decisions

00:12:51.189 --> 00:12:55.180
on the day of and I always try to react
to what I'm

00:12:55.180 --> 00:12:58.660
because things change all the time if
you plan too much you're in for a rude

00:12:58.660 --> 00:13:02.890
awakening because you get to a location
and things always change so I always try

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:07.150
to wait until I'm there and then when
I'm there I make decisions off the cuff

00:13:07.150 --> 00:13:13.990
or a little more you know in a way
that's keeping it fresh and then I

00:13:13.990 --> 00:13:19.300
involve the actors in those decisions in
the DP etc but I would probably try to

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:24.280
be a little more democratic and how I am
reacting to things and my approach

00:13:24.280 --> 00:13:28.030
because I don't have a shot list
generally and I don't have you know a

00:13:28.030 --> 00:13:33.430
lot of dialogue on how I'm doing certain
things so I want to make sure the next

00:13:33.430 --> 00:13:37.000
time I communicate a little better but
as I said it's easy to get into your own

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:41.860
head with these things so what can you
tell us about the history of the Carlyle

00:13:41.860 --> 00:13:47.920
the Carlyle so the history goes back to
about 1930 and it was built right on the

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:53.530
tail end of the 20s and that old boom
and the roaring 20s and the good times

00:13:53.530 --> 00:13:59.140
in New York and the real estate you know
values totally going through the roof

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:03.850
and obviously it ended in a crash but
the conception of the Carlyle and the

00:14:03.850 --> 00:14:09.340
whole notion of it was born during that
era so as it was being built things were

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:14.080
falling apart economically so there was
this whole decision made by Moses

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:18.850
Ginsberg at the time who built it should
we continue should we build it and the

00:14:18.850 --> 00:14:22.120
decision was yes because they would
already broken ground but then they

00:14:22.120 --> 00:14:27.550
ended up they ended up losing it several
months into their mortgage and you know

00:14:27.550 --> 00:14:32.290
they they lost it right after they built
it within a year so and I think New York

00:14:32.290 --> 00:14:36.160
was in a real crisis then I mean the
Empire State Building was erected at the

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:40.060
same time and it was called The Empty
State Building for a decade because no

00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:45.400
one was leasing office space so New York
was in the throes of something quite

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:49.630
unique and status properties like The
Carlyle

00:14:49.630 --> 00:14:55.750
I think suffered during that time but
eventually in the 40s and in the 50s and

00:14:55.750 --> 00:15:00.040
then certainly in the 60s with JFK
coming in there and really making it a

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:05.740
signature as far as you know staying
there and you know Truman stayed there

00:15:05.740 --> 00:15:09.130
but JFK had this
sexiness and the glamour of jackieo and

00:15:09.130 --> 00:15:14.110
they were a bit younger and they had
Camelot and that whole thing was being

00:15:14.110 --> 00:15:18.670
born not only in Washington but at the
Carlyle as well so that really made it

00:15:18.670 --> 00:15:23.350
this place that had this legendary
status and this mythic quality so I was

00:15:23.350 --> 00:15:27.130
always intrigued by that and you know I
love old New York and I think the

00:15:27.130 --> 00:15:31.180
Carlyle just represents all those
different decades and the flavors that

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:37.120
you get and in all those years and I
mean today it's a lot different but it's

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:42.670
holding on to certain aspects of it and
a lot of staff was telling me about this

00:15:42.670 --> 00:15:47.650
place has a patina about it there is a
vintage quality to it and I was trying

00:15:47.650 --> 00:15:52.420
to get to that essence and the heart of
it with the film and I think I got in a

00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:56.110
little bit and a little patch here and
there but ultimately it was a tough

00:15:56.110 --> 00:16:01.150
thing to mine because they also pride
themselves on discretion so you have to

00:16:01.150 --> 00:16:06.160
kind of figure that out as well sure but
I like how you also interwove stories of

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:10.390
the staff and what they could tell and I
thought it was interesting forgive me

00:16:10.390 --> 00:16:14.290
the one gentleman's name and he was the
the man who retired it was there at

00:16:14.290 --> 00:16:18.310
white I think so right and he talked
about how it was such a different

00:16:18.310 --> 00:16:22.960
experience working there years ago and
how the world had changed and his

00:16:22.960 --> 00:16:26.350
position there now didn't feel the same
and I thought that was an interesting

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:32.230
take on how things change over time you
know etiquette changes obviously yeah

00:16:32.230 --> 00:16:38.800
it's true sad actually it is and when he
said it I was I felt it when he said and

00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:43.450
I knew that like sometimes you hear when
a line has said or spoken that you want

00:16:43.450 --> 00:16:46.600
to make sure you get it in the film and
you remember it when it comes time to

00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:51.100
the edit and when he said that like
things have changed and used to go to a

00:16:51.100 --> 00:16:55.240
ballgame and people were dressed and you
would go out on the sidewalk and people

